  "dom_id": "step-q86001",
  "name": "Question 17",
  "problem": "Let $H=\\{1,2,3\\}.$ Which set is equivalent to the following relation on $H?$\n\n$$\nQ=\\{(x,y)\\in {H}^{2}\\,:\\,x+y=5\\}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nIn this case, $Q$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {H}^{2}$ such that $x+y=5.$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in H$ to see which elements $y\\in H$ it could be paired with to satisfy the conditions of our relation.\n\n- If $x=1,$ then there is no value of  $y\\in H$ that satisfies $x+y=5.$\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the value $y=3$ satisfies $x+y=5.$ So, $Q$ contains the ordered pair $(2,3).$\n\n- If $x=3,$ hen the value $y=2$ satisfies $x+y=2.$  So, $Q$ contains the ordered pair $(3,2).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nQ=\\{(2,3),(3,2)\\}\n$$",
